 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Non-rigid Distortion:** If all components (NeRF and DSK) are freely optimized, the NeRF may undergo non-rigid distortion to compensate for blur errors. This is mitigated by the alignment loss $L_{align}$ [S1].
-*   **3D Inconsistency:** Because the blur kernel is optimized pixel-wise independently, it may incur inconsistency in 3D geometry and appearance compared to methods that enforce rigid transformations [S3].
-*   **Positional Encoding:** Using positional encoding on the input pixel coordinate $p$ for the DSK module was tested but found not to help, likely because the spatially-varying kernel changes gradually without high-frequency variation [S1].
+*   **Non-rigid Distortion:** If all components are freely optimized, the NeRF may undergo non-rigid distortion to match the blur. This is mitigated by the alignment loss $L_{align}$ [S1].
+*   **3D Inconsistency:** Pixel-wise independent optimization of the blur kernel can incur inconsistency in 3D geometry and appearance compared to scene-wise shared transformations [S3].
+*   **Positional Encoding on Input:** Using positional encoding on the input $\mathbf{p}$ of $G_\Phi$ was tested but found not to help, likely because the spatially-varying kernel changes gradually without high-frequency variation [S1].
+*   **Gamma Correction:** Simple gamma correction ($1/2.2$) is used to compensate for non-linear CRF. More complex CRFs could be used but were found unnecessary [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is computationally intensive due to the joint optimization of the NeRF and the DSK module. For each pixel, $N$ rays are rendered instead of 1. With $N=5$ and 128 samples per ray, the rendering cost is roughly $5 \times$ that of standard NeRF per pixel.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the NeRF MLP, the DSK MLP, and view embeddings for all input views. The DSK MLP is small (4 layers, 64 channels), so its memory footprint is negligible compared to the NeRF.
-*   **Performance:** On synthetic scenes, DeblurNeRF achieves higher PSNR/SSIM and lower LPIPS than variants without gamma correction, alignment loss, or origin optimization [S1]. It is suited for moderate blur. For severe motion blur, physically grounded methods like BAD-NeRF may recover sharper results and improve pose accuracy, albeit with higher computational complexity [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Does not require sharp ground-truth images; handles both camera motion and defocus blur; efficient approximation for moderate blur.
-*   **Limitations:** Potential 3D inconsistency due to pixel-wise independent blur modeling; less effective for severe blur compared to trajectory-based methods; requires careful tuning of the alignment loss to prevent NeRF distortion.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires rendering $N$ rays per pixel. With $N=5$ and standard sampling (128 samples total), the cost is roughly $5 \times$ the cost of a single ray render per pixel, plus the MLP inference for the DSK.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing parameters for the NeRF and the DSK MLP. The DSK MLP is small (4 layers, 64 channels).
+*   **Performance:** On synthetic scenes, the full model achieves the highest average PSNR/SSIM and lowest LPIPS compared to ablations (w/o gamma, w/o align, w/o origin opt) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles both camera motion blur and defocus blur. Recovers sharp novel views at inference. Compact and efficient approximation suited for moderate blur [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** May suffer from 3D inconsistency due to pixel-wise independent blur modeling [S3]. Less effective for severe motion blur compared to physically grounded methods like BAD-NeRF, which incurs higher computational complexity [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When input images are blurry (motion or defocus) and sharp novel views are desired. Suitable for moderate blur.
+*   **When not to use:** For severe motion blur where physical trajectory modeling is critical, or when 3D geometric consistency across views is paramount over flexibility in blur modeling [S2, S3].
 
 ## Background
-DeblurNeRF (also known as DeRF) was introduced by Xiaoyu Li et al. in 2021/2022 [S1]. It is the first method to recover a sharp NeRF from blurry input images by adopting an analysis-by-synthesis approach. The method was implemented on a PyTorch re-implementation of NeRF and tested on synthetic scenes generated with Blender and real-world scenes captured with a Canon EOS RP [S1]. It differs from DP-NeRF by using pixel-wise independent transformations (DSK) instead of scene-wise shared rigid transformations, which leads to 3D inconsistency but allows for more flexible spatially varying blur modeling [S3].
+DeblurNeRF (also known as DeRF) was proposed by Xiaoyu Li et al. and published in CVPR 2022 [S1]. It is the first method to recover a sharp NeRF from blurry input images using an analysis-by-synthesis approach [S1]. It was implemented on a PyTorch re-implementation of NeRF and tested on synthetic scenes generated with Blender and real-world scenes captured with a Canon EOS RP [S1]. It differs from DP-NeRF by using pixel-wise independent transformations (DSK) instead of scene-wise shared rigid transformations (RBK) [S3].
 
